arXiv:2603.08329v1 [cs.CL] 09 Mar 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">SPD-RAG: Sub-Agent Per Document Retrieval-Augmented Generation</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yagiz Can Akay
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>TOBB University of Economics and Technology
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Muhammed Yusuf Kartal
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>TOBB University of Economics and Technology
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Esra Alparslan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>TOBB University of Economics and Technology
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Faruk Ortakoyluoglu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>TOBB University of Economics and Technology
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Arda Akpinar
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>OSTIM Technical University<span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{y.akay, m.kartal, ealparslan, fortakoyluoglu}@etu.edu.tr</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter"> 230206003@ostimteknik.edu.tr</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Answering complex, real-world queries often requires synthesizing facts scattered across vast document corpora. In these settings, standard retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) pipelines suffer from incomplete evidence coverage, while long-context large language models (LLMs) struggle to reason reliably over massive inputs. We introduce SPD-RAG, a hierarchical multi-agent framework for exhaustive cross-document question answering that decomposes the problem along the document axis. Each document is processed by a dedicated document-level agent operating only on its own content, enabling focused retrieval, while a coordinator dispatches tasks to relevant agents and aggregates their partial answers. Agent outputs are synthesized by merging partial answers through a token-bounded synthesis layer (which supports recursive map-reduce for massive corpora). This document-level specialization with centralized fusion improves scalability and answer quality in heterogeneous multi-document settings while yielding a modular, extensible retrieval pipeline. On the LOONG benchmark (EMNLP 2024) for long-context multi-document QA, SPD-RAG achieves an Avg Score of 58.1 (GPT-5 evaluation), outperforming Normal RAG (33.0) and Agentic RAG (32.8) while using only 38% of the API cost of a full-context baseline (68.0).</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) and the agentic systems built around them are increasingly used for complex information search tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">guo2024large</span></cite>. Real-world questions often require synthesizing evidence scattered across many documents, such as assessing a company’s financial risks across years of reports or integrating findings from multiple scientific papers.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          Examples inspired by scenarios in the Loong benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">wang2024leave</span></cite>.</span></span></span> Such questions are natural and common, yet expose critical coverage and reasoning bottlenecks in current NLP systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional single-index or single-agent retrieval systems struggle to maintain both scalability and contextual relevance over large, heterogeneous corpora, especially for long or structurally complex documents. Standard RAG pipelines retrieve a fixed number of documents <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and process them within a single context window <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">lewis2020retrieval</span></cite>, which fails when answers depend on information distributed across many documents, since evidence beyond the top-<math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> results is typically discarded in a single retrieval pass. Long-context LLMs extend context windows to 128K–2M tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">team2024gemini</span></cite>, but empirical evidence shows that reasoning quality degrades significantly as context length increases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">liu2023lost</span></cite>. This suggests that the bottleneck is not only retrieval, but also <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reasoning at scale</span> over many documents and hundreds of thousands of tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduce <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SPD-RAG</span> (Sub-agent per Document Retrieval-Augmented Generation), a hierarchical multi-agent architecture for <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">exhaustive</span> multi-document question answering that factors the problem along the document axis rather than the task axis. Instead of forcing a single model to hunt through a massive global index, <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SPD-RAG</span> uses a central coordinator to decompose the user’s query into shared instructions. It then deploys a dedicated, cost-efficient sub-agent to <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">each</span> document in the corpus. These agents operate entirely in parallel, treating their assigned documents as isolated retrieval universes to extract relevant findings. Finally, a synthesis model aggregates these document-grounded findings—employing a recursive merging fallback for exceptionally large corpora—to construct a comprehensive, final answer. Our codebase and datasets used in this paper can be found at 
<br class="ltx_break"><a href="https://github.com/NebulAICompany/SPD-RAG" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/NebulAICompany/SPD-RAG</a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SPD-RAG</span>, a hierarchical multi-agent framework that combines per-document agentic RAG with a centralized synthesis layer, where cost-efficient document agents are coordinated by a smarter coordinator agent, enabling document-level specialization and parallel execution while allowing each document to be analyzed in depth to ensure that all relevant information is incorporated without missing critical evidence.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on the Loong benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">wang2024leave</span></cite>, which includes multi-document QA instances over financial reports and academic papers with an average of 11 documents per instance and context lengths from 10K to beyond 250K tokens, and show that our system substantially outperforms standard RAG and Agentic RAG baselines under GPT-5-judged Avg Score (adapted from Loong’s GPT-4-judged protocol; see §<a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Evaluation Metrics ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ SPD-RAG: Sub-Agent Per Document Retrieval-Augmented Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>), with gains of around +25 points (76% higher average score) compared to Normal RAG and Agentic RAG baselines.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze ablations, document type and task complexity effects, and cost–quality tradeoffs, showing that <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SPD-RAG</span> attains over 85% of full-context baseline quality at roughly 38% of the API cost.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>LLM-Based Multi-Agent Systems</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM-based multi-agent systems are increasingly used for complex task solving. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">guo2024large</span></cite> provide a comprehensive survey of agent profiles, communication protocols, and collaboration strategies. Hierarchical architectures such as MegaAgent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">wang2024megaagent</span></cite>, which demonstrates autonomous cooperation among up to 590 agents via multi-level task decomposition, and AgentOrchestra <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">zhang2025agentorchestra</span></cite>, which achieves state-of-the-art performance on GAIA using a central planning agent and specialized sub-agents, exemplify this trend.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Scaling laws for multi-agent systems offer quantitative guidance for architecture design. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">kim2025towards</span></cite> evaluate 180 configurations across five architectures, finding that centralized coordination yields an 80.9% improvement on parallelizable tasks, but that multi-agent overhead grows superlinearly. Their result that independent agents amplify errors by 17.2<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> compared to 4.4<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> for centralized systems directly motivates our coordinator-based design. The taxonomy of hierarchical multi-agent systems by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">moore2025taxonomy</span></cite> further classifies design patterns along five axes: control hierarchy, information flow, role delegation, temporal layering, and communication structure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Long Document and Multi-Document QA</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Long documents that exceed LLM context windows are often handled via divide-and-conquer strategies. LLM<math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>MapReduce <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">zhou2025llm</span></cite> formalizes two key challenges: <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inter-chunk dependency</span> (one chunk depends on context from another) and <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inter-chunk conflict</span> (chunks provide contradictory information), and proposes structured information protocols and in-context confidence calibration to mitigate them. ToM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">guo2025tom</span></cite> extends this approach with a hierarchical DocTree representation for recursive reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For multi-document QA, LongAgent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">zhao2024longagent</span></cite> splits a 128K-token document into chunks assigned to member agents, with a leader agent orchestrating discussions and inter-member communication to reduce hallucinations. However, LongAgent targets a <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">single</span> long document rather than <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multiple independent</span> documents that may conflict. DocAgent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">sun2025docagent</span></cite> uses a multi-agent framework that mimics human reading via a tree-structured outline and reviewer agent, while MDocAgent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">han2025mdocagent</span></cite> employs five specialized agents for multi-modal document understanding.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Hierarchical and Recursive Summarization</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our hierarchical merging mechanism builds on tree-based retrieval and recursive summarization. RAPTOR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">sarthi2024raptor</span></cite> recursively embeds, clusters, and summarizes text chunks to construct a bottom-up tree with multiple abstraction levels; retrieving from the appropriate level yields a 20% absolute accuracy gain on QuALITY. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">ou2025context</span></cite> show that hierarchical merging can amplify hallucinations during recursive summarization and propose context-aware augmentation strategies that replace intermediate summaries with relevant input context, which we adopt in our merging protocol.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Graph-Based Document Analysis</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Graph-based document analysis is particularly effective for global sensemaking queries. GraphRAG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">edge2024local</span></cite> constructs a knowledge graph of entities and relations from source documents and applies hierarchical community detection (e.g., the Leiden algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">traag2019louvain</span></cite>) to obtain multi-level communities with textual summaries. At query time, it retrieves relevant communities, elicits community-level partial answers, and aggregates them via a map–reduce–style procedure. A survey on LLM-empowered knowledge graph construction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">bian2025llm</span></cite> highlights how LLMs are reshaping the classical knowledge graph pipeline across three key stages: ontology engineering, knowledge extraction, and knowledge fusion. Our work is complementary: instead of inducing an explicit knowledge graph, <span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SPD-RAG</span> operates directly over agent-produced textual summaries and performs similarity-guided recursive synthesis across many documents.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5 </span>Benchmarks for Multi-Document Reasoning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Loong benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">wang2024leave</span></cite> evaluates long-context LLMs on extended multi-document QA where every document is relevant ("Leave No Document Behind") across financial, legal, and academic domains in English and Chinese. It spans four task types with context lengths from 10K to over 250K tokens, and even strong frontier models achieve only modest Avg Scores and medium perfect-answer rates, underscoring the difficulty of reasoning over many long, all-relevant documents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Complementary datasets probe other aspects of multi-document reasoning. MoNaCo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">wolfson2025monaco</span></cite> focuses on natural questions over dozens of Wikipedia pages, MEBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">lin2025mebench</span></cite> on high-entity-density QA, FanOutQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">zhu2024fanoutqa</span></cite> on multi-hop questions with long cross-document evidence chains, and HoloBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">maekawa2024holistic</span></cite> on database-style aggregation over unstructured text. Taken together, these benchmarks show that scalable multi-document reasoning remains an open challenge, with Loong emphasizing the especially demanding all-relevant, long-context regime.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ SPD-RAG: Sub-Agent Per Document Retrieval-Augmented Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents an overview of the <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SPD-RAG</span> architecture. The system processes a query through three layers: the Coordination Layer (§<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Coordination Layer ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ SPD-RAG: Sub-Agent Per Document Retrieval-Augmented Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), the Parallel Retrieval Layer (§<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Parallel Retrieval Layer ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ SPD-RAG: Sub-Agent Per Document Retrieval-Augmented Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), and the Synthesis Layer (§<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Synthesis Layer ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ SPD-RAG: Sub-Agent Per Document Retrieval-Augmented Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.08329v1/../images/architecture.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:486/188;" width="486" height="188" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of <span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SPD-RAG</span> Architecture.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Coordination Layer</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a query <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and corpus <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, the system first processes the query through a coordinator agent. The coordinator’s role is to decompose the user query into a <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Shared Instruction Set for Documents</span> and <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Synthesis Directives</span>. It generates a <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">WriteTodos</span> structured object containing (a) a list of <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sub_agent_todos</span>: atomic, self-contained extraction tasks specifying exactly what fields, entities, or numeric values to extract (the shared instructions); and (b) a <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">synthesis_directive</span> (2–4 sentences) instructing the downstream Synthesis Layer on how to prioritize and structure the merged response.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Parallel Retrieval Layer</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assign a dedicated sub-agent <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to each document <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in the corpus. Each sub-agent operates as an independent RAG loop over its assigned document, guided by the shared instruction set from the Coordination Layer.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Document-Scoped Retrieval and Reasoning.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each document <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as an isolated retrieval universe; the assigned sub-agent is strictly constrained to search tool calls within <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, preventing cross-document distractor chunks from degrading local extraction. Each sub-agent receives the original query <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, its assigned document name, and the shared instruction set (todo list), then operates in an iterative retrieve-and-reason loop. On each turn, the agent either issues a <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">search</span> action (a focused query string) or a <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">finalize</span> action that terminates the loop and emits the document-grounded findings. The sub-agent prompt instructs the agent to attempt at least 2 focused searches before concluding a piece of information is absent, with a total cap of 5 search calls across all tasks. Each <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">search</span> action triggers dense vector retrieval over the sub-agent’s dedicated per-document Qdrant index. This retrieval uses Cohere <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">embed-v4.0</span> embeddings (1536-dimensional, cosine similarity) to fetch the top <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=15</annotation></semantics></math> most similar chunks. These chunks are then re-ranked by Cohere <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rerank-v4.0-fast</span> to yield a final set of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{top\_n}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>top_n</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{top\_n}=5</annotation></semantics></math> chunks per search. The documents are split into chunks offline using LangChain’s <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</span> in Markdown mode with a chunk size of 1000 tokens and 250-token overlap.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Findings Output.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We denote the findings of each sub-agent as</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}=\alpha_{i}(q,d_{i})=\langle s_{i},r_{i}\rangle" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}=\alpha_{i}(q,d_{i})=\langle s_{i},r_{i}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the natural language findings report from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is a scalar relevance/confidence score. These outputs are passed to the Synthesis Layer.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Parallelism.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All RAG loops for the documents are dispatched concurrently via LangGraph’s <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Send</span> API fan-out, which emits one <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Send("document_sub_agent_node", ...)</span> per document and executes all targets in parallel within LangGraph’s async runtime.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Synthesis Layer</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While modern LLMs feature extensive context windows, synthesizing hundreds of document reports can still exceed these limits or cause ’lost in the middle’ degradation. To future-proof the architecture for massive corpora, the Synthesis Layer is designed as a dynamic map-reduce pipeline that aggregates findings recursively.
The Synthesis Layer receives the synthesis directives from the Coordination Layer and the findings <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{o_{1},o_{2},\ldots,o_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o_{1},o_{2},\ldots,o_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from the Parallel Retrieval Layer. It aggregates and synthesizes the findings recursively through a similarity-ordered process until the total findings size falls within the target context size to produce the final output.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Similarity-Ordered Merge &amp; Synthesis.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In iteration <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we maintain a set of findings/summaries <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}^{(t)}=\{s^{(t)}_{1},\ldots,s^{(t)}_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}^{(t)}=\{s^{(t)}_{1},\ldots,s^{(t)}_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We embed each summary using Cohere <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">embed-v4.0</span> and compute a cosine-similarity matrix via <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sklearn.metrics.pairwise.cosine_similarity</span>. This is converted to a distance matrix <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{ij}=1-\cos(s^{(t)}_{i},s^{(t)}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{ij}=1-\cos(s^{(t)}_{i},s^{(t)}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is passed to <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AgglomerativeClustering</span> with <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">n_clusters=1</span>, <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">linkage="average"</span> (UPGMA), producing a complete dendrogram over all current summaries. We traverse the merge tree bottom-up, greedily accumulating nodes into a batch as long as the total <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tiktoken</span> count does not exceed the target budget of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=750{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>750,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=750{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> tokens. Semantically similar summaries are grouped first, so each batch contains the most similar available summaries within the token limit.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Aggregate and Synthesize Findings.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each batch, an LLM is prompted with the concatenated findings, the synthesis directive, and the original query <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> to produce an aggregated and synthesized summary. All batch synthesis calls within an iteration are issued concurrently via <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">asyncio.gather</span>. The merged summaries form the input for the next iteration <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}^{(t+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}^{(t+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The same <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Synthesis</span> prompt used at every level of the merging tree is applied even at the final level—there is no separate synthesis-only prompt.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Context Size Condition and Output.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The layer evaluates whether the total findings size exceeds the context size threshold (i.e., <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}^{(t+1)}|&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}^{(t+1)}|&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>). If the condition is true, the process loops back to the similarity-ordered merge and synthesis step. If the condition is false (the total findings size is within the context size, leaving a single final summary <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>), the process terminates and yields <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> as the final output. If <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}^{(t+1)}|\geq|\mathcal{S}^{(t)}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}^{(t+1)}|\geq|\mathcal{S}^{(t)}|</annotation></semantics></math> (no-progress case), the entire current level is forced into a single batch to guarantee termination.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Context Size Condition and Output. ‣ 3.3 Synthesis Layer ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ SPD-RAG: Sub-Agent Per Document Retrieval-Augmented Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> gives the pseudocode for the general recursive synthesis procedure.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Recursive Synthesis</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
                
                
               Findings <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}=\{s_{1},\ldots,s_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}=\{s_{1},\ldots,s_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, query <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>,  synthesis directive <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, context size budget <math id="alg1.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
                
                
               Final Output <math id="alg1.l0a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
                
                
               <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\leftarrow\textsc{CohereEmbed}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">CohereEmbed</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\leftarrow\textsc{CohereEmbed}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\leftarrow 1-\textsc{CosineSimilarity}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">CosineSimilarity</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\leftarrow 1-\textsc{CosineSimilarity}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{tree}\leftarrow\textsc{AgglomerativeCluster}(" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">tree</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">AgglomerativeCluster</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{tree}\leftarrow\textsc{AgglomerativeCluster}(</annotation></semantics></math>   <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D,\;\textit{linkage}=\text{``average''})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">linkage</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>“average”</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D,\;\textit{linkage}=\text{``average''})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{batches}\leftarrow\textsc{GroupByTokens}(" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">batches</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">GroupByTokens</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{batches}\leftarrow\textsc{GroupByTokens}(</annotation></semantics></math>   <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S},\;\textit{tree},\;B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">tree</mtext><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S},\;\textit{tree},\;B)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\textit{batches}|\geq|\mathcal{S}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">batches</mtext><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\textit{batches}|\geq|\mathcal{S}|</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
                
                
                 <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{batches}\leftarrow[\mathcal{S}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">batches</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{batches}\leftarrow[\mathcal{S}]</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\leftarrow\textsc{AsyncGather}(" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">AsyncGather</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\leftarrow\textsc{AsyncGather}(</annotation></semantics></math>   <math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Synthesize}(b,q,d)\ \forall b\in\textit{batches})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Synthesize</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.667em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">batches</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Synthesize}(b,q,d)\ \forall b\in\textit{batches})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
                
                
               <span id="alg1.l10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
                
                
               <span id="alg1.l11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}[0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}[0]</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Behavior on Loong.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, with Gemini 2.5 Pro’s 1M-token context window and a budget of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=750{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>750,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=750{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, the combined sub-agent findings for all instances in our Loong evaluation set fit within a single batch, so the conditional check allowed output after one iteration. The recursive loop is therefore a design capability intended for much larger corpora, where total findings size genuinely exceeds the context size.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Dataset</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SPD-RAG</span> on the Loong benchmark  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">wang2024leave</span></cite>. Loong is an extended multi-document QA instances in both English and Chinese, with an average of 11 documents per test case spanning two real-world scenarios: financial reports and academic papers. Each test case is constructed so that every document is relevant to the final answer ("Leave No Document Behind", meaning a correct response requires synthesizing evidence from all provided documents), and is annotated under one of four task types: Spotlight Locating, Comparison, Clustering, or Chain of Reasoning, with context lengths ranging from 10K to beyond 250K tokens. We evaluate on the <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">English</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Set 4 (200k-250k tokens)</span> portion of the benchmark only. Our evaluation set comprises 40 instances involving academic papers and 62 involving financial reports (102 instances total), with the four task types distributed as: Spotlight Locating (27), Comparison (15), Clustering (49), and Chain of Reasoning (11).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare against the following systems, all backed by Gemini 2.5 Pro:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline (Full Context)</span>: All documents for each instance are concatenated and provided directly in the context window of Gemini 2.5 Pro, representing the oracle long-context upper bound.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Normal RAG</span>: Standard vector search top-<math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> retrieval over the full document corpus, followed by LLM reasoning over the retrieved chunks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agentic RAG</span>: A single-agent ReAct-style RAG system (implemented via LangGraph/LangChain) that can issue multiple iterative retrieval calls over the full global corpus. It uses the same embedding and re-ranking pipeline as <span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SPD-RAG</span>, but lacks per-document specialization.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report the following metrics, following the Loong evaluation protocol <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">wang2024leave</span></cite>:</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg Score</span>: An LLM-judged score (0–100) measuring the degree to which the predicted answer accurately covers the gold answer, following the evaluation protocol of Loong <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">wang2024leave</span>)</cite>. <span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                    <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                    
                    
                    
                  Because the original legacy GPT-4 model API is unavailable for new scaled evaluations, we adopt GPT-5 as the evaluator. Recent literature increasingly adopts GPT-5 as the standard for LLM-as-a-judge protocols due to its superior alignment with human grading <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">wolfson2025monaco</span>)</cite>. While our baseline numbers are re-computed under this GPT-5 judge for strict fairness, raw scores may not be perfectly 1-to-1 comparable with the originally published Loong leaderboard.</span></span></span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Perfect Rate (PR%)</span>: Percentage of queries where the system receives a perfect score of 100.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg Token Usage</span>: Mean per-query total (input + output) token usage.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg Cost (USD)</span>: Mean per-query API cost, enabling cost-efficiency comparisons.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg Latency (s)</span>: Mean per-query wall-clock time.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Implementation Details</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All systems use <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemini 2.5 Pro</span> (temperature 0.0) as the backbone LLM for the coordinator, merging layer, and synthesis layer. <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SPD-RAG</span> document sub-agents use <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemini 2.5 Flash</span> for document-scoped retrieval, reducing per-document cost while preserving reasoning quality at the merging and synthesis stages. GPT-5 is used exclusively as the LLM judge for the evaluation of the Avg score and is not part of the pipeline itself.
Prompts for all systems, including baselines, were optimized for information extraction and synthesis to ensure fair comparison. Full prompt templates for all systems are provided in the code repository.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Indexing.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each document is pre-indexed into a dedicated Qdrant vector collection using LangChain’s <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</span> (Implementation details are provided in the Appendix.).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Retrieval per sub-agent.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">search</span> tool call performs dense vector retrieval over the document index, returning the top-<math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=15</annotation></semantics></math>) chunks using cosine similarity in Qdrant. These candidates are subsequently re-ranked with Cohere <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rerank-v4.0-fast</span>, and the top <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{top\_n}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>top_n</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{top\_n}=5</annotation></semantics></math> chunks are passed to the sub-agent LLM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Merging layer.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The token budget per merge batch is <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=750{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>750,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=750{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> tokens (75% of Gemini 2.5 Pro’s 1M context cap). Agglomerative clustering uses <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sklearn</span> with UPGMA linkage and precomputed cosine-distance matrix.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Parallelism.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Document agents run concurrently via LangGraph <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Send</span> API fan-out. Batch synthesis calls within each merge iteration are parallelized with <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">asyncio.gather</span>. Synchronous Cohere API calls are offloaded to the thread pool via <span id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">asyncio.to_thread</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Safety limits.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each document sub-agent’s iterative retrieval loop is capped at 5 iterations; the Agentic RAG baseline caps at 10 iterations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cost tracking.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Per-query costs are computed from LangSmith run metadata (total token counts <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> per-token pricing.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Main Results ‣ 5 Results ‣ SPD-RAG: Sub-Agent Per Document Retrieval-Augmented Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports the main results across all systems on our Loong evaluation set. Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.1 Main Results ‣ 5 Results ‣ SPD-RAG: Sub-Agent Per Document Retrieval-Augmented Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates these performance differences across the four specific task types.</p>
</div>
